, "newValue": "Yeshiva University, established in 1886 in New York City, represents a significant fusion of Jewish education and higher learning, marking a pivotal moment in the integration of Jewish values within the broader context of American academia. Founded by a group of Orthodox Jewish leaders, including Rabbi Isaac Elchanan Spektor, Yeshiva University was the first institution of higher learning under Jewish auspices in the United States. The university emerged during a period when many Jewish immigrants, facing cultural and religious challenges, sought to preserve their heritage while adapting to American society, thus contributing to the shaping of modern Jewish identity in North America.

The university's curriculum has evolved over the decades, blending rigorous secular studies with traditional Jewish scholarship, which has allowed it to produce notable alumni, including Nobel laureates, influential rabbis, and leaders in various fields. Yeshiva University officially became a university in 1945 and has grown to encompass multiple schools, including the Bennington School of Law and the Sy Syms School of Business, reflecting its commitment to comprehensive education. The establishment of the university's medical school in 1970 further exemplified its ambition to contribute to the health sciences, addressing both Jewish and wider societal needs in the process.

